<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Sulpicius Quirinius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 21 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Quirinius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Sulpicius Quirinius was a Roman senator, consul in 12 BCE, who as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> carried out a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> in 6 CE, when it became a Roman province after the deposition of Archelaus. The census provoked the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-of-galilee">Judas of Galilee</a> and is mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 21 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/publius-sulpicius-quirinius.jpg" alt="Publius Sulpicius Quirinius" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Church of the Holy Saviour in Chora, Istanbul · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meister_der_Kahriye-Cami-Kirche_in_Istanbul_005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Quirinius was born around 51 BCE into an undistinguished family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the son and grandson of men named Publius Sulpicius Quirinius. He followed the usual path of service for an ambitious young man of his class. According to the historian Florus, he defeated the Marmaridae, a tribe of desert raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, around 14 BCE, possibly while governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and Cyrene, but declined the honorific name Marmaricus. His election as consul in 12 BCE was a sign of the favour of Augustus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 5 to 3 BCE Quirinius led a campaign, probably as legate of Galatia, against the Homonadenses, a tribe of the mountains of Galatia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>. He reduced their strongholds and starved out the defenders, and was awarded a triumph and elected duumvir by the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a>. By 1 CE he was tutor to Augustus' grandson Gaius Caesar, and when Augustus' support shifted to his stepson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, Quirinius changed allegiance too. His first marriage, to Claudia Appia, ended in divorce, as did his second, to Aemilia Lepida, in 20 CE he accused Lepida of falsely claiming that he had fathered her son and of trying to poison him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says she was popular and the public saw his prosecution as spiteful.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus">Herod Archelaus</a> was banished in 6 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> came under direct Roman rule with Coponius as prefect, and Quirinius was appointed legate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with instructions to assess Judaea for taxation, beginning with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Censuses were forbidden under Jewish law and the assessment was greatly resented. Open revolt was prevented only by the high priest Joazar, but according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> the census led to the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-of-galilee">Judas of Galilee</a> and the party of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zealots">Zealots</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Quirinius governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, until 12 CE and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>. He died nine years later. Tiberius asked the Senate for a public funeral, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> calls him a tireless soldier who became consul under Augustus. His governorship is confirmed by coins of his administration dated to the 36th year of Caesar, and the Stele of Quintus Aemilius Secundus, an officer who served under him, records a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of the city of Apamea ordered by Quirinius.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, chapter 2, links the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of Quirinius. This is widely held to contradict the Gospel of Matthew, which places the birth in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, who died in 4 BCE, and most critical scholars judge Luke inconsistent with the historical evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quirinius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Judea became a Roman province under equestrian prefects, and the census of <b>Quirinius</b> that followed provoked the revolt of Judas of Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-ethnarchy-of-judea">Herodian Ethnarchy of Judea</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Roman rule they were tributaries of the Roman Empire, but they raided settled lands when they could; Roman commanders, including <b>Publius Sulpicius Quirinius</b> under Augustus, campaigned against them.</blockquote><a href="/state/marmaridae">Marmaridae</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanuvium">Lanuvium</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=11" aria-label="Lanuvium on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4859 679 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Publius Sulpicius Quirinius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 949h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 949h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.68,12.7&amp;year=11">What was here in 11 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section><h2 class="enc-section-title">Mentioned in 2 state histories</h2><div class="enc-mentions"><a href="/state/marmaridae">Marmaridae</a><a href="/state/herodian-ethnarchy-of-judea">Herodian Ethnarchy of Judea</a></div></section>
